/** @mainpage Qore ml Module

    @tableofcontents

    @section mlintro ml Module Introduction

    The \c ml module provides machine learning algorithms for %Qore programs, enabling
    automated detection of anomalies, discovery of natural groupings, forecasting of future
    values, and simplification of complex data — all thread-safe and without external
    dependencies like Python.

    This module wraps the Eigen C++ linear algebra library to provide efficient, native
    implementations of common ML algorithms. Optional ONNX Runtime support allows running
    pre-trained models from frameworks like PyTorch, scikit-learn, and TensorFlow.

    @section mlalgorithms Available Algorithms

    @subsection mlalgo_table Algorithm Overview

    |Algorithm|Type|Use Case|
    |---|---|---|
    |@ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest"|Anomaly Detection|Flag fraudulent transactions, sensor malfunctions, or intrusions|
    |@ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF"|Anomaly Detection|Find purchases unusual for a specific customer segment or region|
    |@ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN"|Clustering|Discover delivery zones, support ticket themes, or traffic hotspots|
    |@ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans"|Clustering|Segment customers into tiers or classify products into categories|
    |@ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM"|Clustering|Identify overlapping segments (e.g., 70%% budget / 30%% impulse buyer)|
    |@ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression"|Regression|Predict property prices, delivery times, or demand from features|
    |@ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters"|Time Series|Forecast monthly revenue, daily server load, or weekly inventory|
    |@ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition"|Time Series|Separate sales into growth trend, holiday spikes, and noise|
    |@ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA"|Dimensionality Reduction|Compress 50 sensor readings into 3 key values for analysis|
    |@ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel"|Inference|Run neural networks or gradient-boosted models trained in Python|

    @subsection mlalgo_anomaly Anomaly Detection

    Catch fraudulent transactions, equipment failures, and security breaches by automatically
    identifying data points that don't fit normal patterns.

    - @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest" — Detects globally unusual records by measuring
      how easy they are to separate from the rest. A \$50,000 transaction when most are under \$500
      is isolated quickly and scores near 1.0 (highly anomalous). Best for broad screening across
      all data. Uses random partitioning; anomalous points are isolated in fewer splits.
    - @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF" — Detects records that are unusual relative to their local neighborhood.
      A \$200 purchase may be normal for premium customers but anomalous for budget customers; LOF
      catches this by comparing local density. Best when "unusual" depends on context. Uses
      k-nearest neighbors to measure local density deviation.

    @subsection mlalgo_clustering Clustering

    Discover customer segments, group related events, and classify data points — either into
    hard groups or with probability scores reflecting genuine uncertainty.

    - @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN" — Discovers natural groups without requiring you to specify how
      many exist. Groups can have any shape: geographic delivery zones, clusters of related
      support tickets, or network traffic hotspots. Points that don't belong to any group are
      labeled as noise.
    - @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans" — Segments data into a specified number of groups — e.g., divide
      10,000 customers into 5 spending tiers or classify products into 3 price categories. Uses
      k-means++ initialization and supports online (mini-batch) updates so segments evolve as
      new data arrives.
    - @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM" — Assigns each record a probability for each group rather than a hard
      label. A customer might be 70%% "budget shopper" and 30%% "impulse buyer," reflecting
      genuine overlap. Uses Gaussian mixtures (EM algorithm) and supports full, diagonal, and
      spherical covariance types.

    @subsection mlalgo_regression Regression

    Predict prices, estimate delivery times, and forecast demand by learning relationships
    between input features and a target value.

    - @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" — Learns the best straight-line relationship
      between inputs and a target. For example, predict a property's price from square footage,
      number of bedrooms, and neighborhood — the model reports which features matter most
      (coefficients), the baseline (intercept), and prediction accuracy (R-squared).

    @subsection mlalgo_timeseries Time Series

    Forecast future values and understand what drives change in data with seasonal patterns,
    such as monthly revenue, daily server load, or weekly inventory levels.

    - @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters" — Forecasts values by learning trend (going up or down)
      and seasonal patterns (e.g., revenue peaks every December, server load spikes Monday
      mornings). Supports additive and multiplicative seasonality with optional damped trends.
    - @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition" — Separates a time series into three
      parts: the underlying growth trend, the repeating seasonal pattern (e.g., holiday shopping
      cycles), and random fluctuations. Helps answer "is this increase due to real growth or just
      a seasonal spike?" Supports additive and multiplicative models.

    @subsection mlalgo_dimreduce Dimensionality Reduction

    Simplify complex data for visualization, noise removal, and faster downstream analysis.

    - @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA" — Compresses many fields into fewer combined values that capture the
      most important patterns. For example, reduce 50 sensor readings to 3 key values that
      explain 95%% of the variance, making it practical to visualize equipment health on a 3D
      plot. Supports fixed component count or automatic selection via variance threshold.

    @subsection mlalgo_onnx ONNX Model Inference

    Run complex models (neural networks, gradient-boosted trees, etc.) trained in Python, R, or
    other frameworks without needing those tools at runtime.

    When compiled with ONNX Runtime support, the module provides:
    - @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel" — Loads and runs pre-trained ONNX models for inference.
      For example, a credit scoring model trained in scikit-learn can be exported to ONNX and
      deployed in a %Qore pipeline for real-time scoring. Supports float, double, int32, and
      int64 tensor types. ONNX Runtime sessions are thread-safe for concurrent inference.

    To check ONNX availability at runtime:
    @code{.py}
hash<Qore::ML::MLCapabilities> caps = Qore::ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (caps.has_onnx) {
    Qore::ML::OnnxModel model("/path/to/model.onnx");
    hash<auto> result = model.run({"input": data});
}
    @endcode

    @section mlchoosing Choosing the Right Algorithm

    <b>"I need to find bad or unusual records"</b>
    - Do you want to catch records that are unusual across <i>all</i> your data?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest"
    - Do you want to catch records that are unusual <i>compared to similar records</i>?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF"

    <b>"I need to group or segment my data"</b>
    - Do you know how many groups you want?
      - Yes → Use @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans"
      - No → Use @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN"
    - Do records need probability scores for multiple groups (soft assignment)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM"

    <b>"I need to predict or forecast values"</b>
    - Is your data a time series with seasonal patterns (daily, weekly, monthly)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters"
    - Do you want to predict a value from input features (price from size, time from distance)?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression"

    <b>"I need to understand or simplify my data"</b>
    - Do you want to separate trend, seasonality, and noise in a time series?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition"
    - Do you want to compress many fields into fewer meaningful values?
      → Use @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA"

    <b>"I have a pre-trained model from Python or R"</b>
    - Export it to ONNX format and use @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel"

    This module is released under the MIT license (see \c COPYING.MIT in the source distribution for
    more information). The module is tagged as such in the module's header (meaning it can be loaded
    unconditionally regardless of how the %Qore library was initialized).

    To use the module in a %Qore script, use the \c %%requires directive as follows:
    @code %requires ml @endcode

    @section mlthread Thread Safety

    All classes in this module are designed for thread-safe usage:
    - Each class instance has independent state (no shared mutable globals)
    - After fitting, scoring/prediction methods can be called concurrently from
      multiple threads
    - Each instance uses its own random number generator
    - ONNX Runtime sessions are inherently thread-safe for inference

    @section mlexamples Usage Examples

    @subsection mlexample_iforest Fraud Detection with IsolationForest

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Train on 30 days of legitimate transactions
Qore::ML::IsolationForest fraud_detector({"n_trees": 100, "sample_size": 256, "threshold": 0.6});
fraud_detector.fit(legitimate_transactions);

# Score an incoming transaction: {amount: 49500.0, hour: 3, merchant_category: 12}
hash<Qore::ML::IsolationForestResult> result = fraud_detector.score(transaction);
if (result.is_anomaly) {
    printf("Suspicious transaction! Score: %f\n", result.anomaly_score);
    # score near 1.0 = highly unusual; flag for review
}
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_linreg Property Price Prediction with Linear Regression

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Train on historical sales: square_footage, bedrooms, distance_to_center -> sale_price
Qore::ML::LinearRegression price_model({"fit_intercept": True});
price_model.fit(historical_sales, "sale_price");

# Predict price for a new listing
hash<Qore::ML::LinearRegressionResult> result = price_model.predict(
    {"square_footage": 1850, "bedrooms": 3, "distance_to_center": 4.2}
);
printf("Estimated price: $%.0f (model accuracy R²: %f)\n", result.prediction, result.r_squared);
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_gmm Customer Segmentation with GMM

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Segment customers by monthly_spend, visit_frequency, avg_order_value
Qore::ML::GMM segmenter({"n_components": 3, "seed": 42});
segmenter.fit(customer_profiles);

hash<Qore::ML::GMMResult> result = segmenter.predict(
    {"monthly_spend": 150.0, "visit_frequency": 8, "avg_order_value": 18.75}
);
printf("Segment: %d (confidence: %.0f%%)\n", result.cluster, result.max_probability * 100);
printf("Segment probabilities: %y\n", result.probabilities);
# e.g., {0: 0.72, 1: 0.21, 2: 0.07} — mostly "regular", some "premium" overlap
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_lof Contextual Anomaly Detection with LOF

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

# Train on normal sensor readings grouped by equipment type
Qore::ML::LOF sensor_monitor({"k": 20, "threshold": 1.5});
sensor_monitor.fit(normal_sensor_readings);

# A reading of 90°F is normal outdoors but anomalous for a server room
hash<Qore::ML::LOFResult> result = sensor_monitor.score(
    {"temperature": 90.0, "humidity": 45.0, "equipment_type": 3}
);
if (result.is_anomaly) {
    printf("Anomaly for this equipment context! LOF score: %f\n", result.lof_score);
}
    @endcode

    @subsection mlexample_onnx Credit Scoring with an ONNX Model

    @code{.py}
%requires ml

hash<Qore::ML::MLCapabilities> caps = Qore::ML::ml_get_capabilities();
if (!caps.has_onnx) {
    printf("ONNX Runtime not available\n");
} else {
    # Load a gradient-boosted model trained in Python/scikit-learn
    Qore::ML::OnnxModel credit_model("/models/credit_score_v2.onnx");

    hash<Qore::ML::OnnxModelInfo> info = credit_model.getModelInfo();
    printf("Model: %s (version %d)\n", info.producer_name, info.version);

    # Score a loan application: income, debt_ratio, credit_history_months, ...
    hash<auto> result = credit_model.run({"X": ((75000.0, 0.28, 120.0),)});
    printf("Credit score: %y\n", result);
}
    @endcode

    @section mlreleasenotes ml Module Release Notes

    @subsection ml_1_0 ml Module Version 1.0
    - Initial release with 9 native ML algorithms:
      - @ref Qore::ML::IsolationForest "IsolationForest" — anomaly detection using isolation trees
      - @ref Qore::ML::LOF "LOF" — Local Outlier Factor density-based anomaly detection
      - @ref Qore::ML::DBSCAN "DBSCAN" — density-based clustering
      - @ref Qore::ML::KMeans "KMeans" — k-means clustering with online updates
      - @ref Qore::ML::GMM "GMM" — Gaussian Mixture Model probabilistic clustering
      - @ref Qore::ML::LinearRegression "LinearRegression" — ordinary least squares regression
      - @ref Qore::ML::HoltWinters "HoltWinters" — triple exponential smoothing forecasting
      - @ref Qore::ML::SeasonalDecomposition "SeasonalDecomposition" — time series decomposition
      - @ref Qore::ML::PCA "PCA" — principal component analysis
    - Optional @ref Qore::ML::OnnxModel "OnnxModel" for ONNX Runtime inference (requires libonnxruntime)
    - All algorithms use Eigen3 for efficient matrix operations
    - \c ml_get_capabilities() function for runtime capability and ONNX support discovery
